s://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/promop/commit/389bac385b362ab6598d5e5b98ac04761fcdac2b","node_id":"C_kwDOPw6uhdoAKDM4OWJhYzM4NWIzNjJhYjY1OThkNWU1Yjk4YWMwNDc2MWZjZGFjMmI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/promop/commit/ea6aa91bfc847e05e68a8e9f67582ddbdc717c61","sha":"ea6aa91bfc847e05e68a8e9f67582ddbdc717c61","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/commits/ea6aa91bfc847e05e68a8e9f67582ddbdc717c61"}],"sha":"389bac385b362ab6598d5e5b98ac04761fcdac2b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/commits/389bac385b362ab6598d5e5b98ac04761fcdac2b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/commits/ea6aa91bfc847e05e68a8e9f67582ddbdc717c61/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T13:56:32Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T13:56:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Let a browser make a conditional write (#1602)\n\nConditional writes went in with #1312 and have never worked from a browser.\n`CORS_ALLOW_HEADERS` is `default_headers` plus two provenance headers, and\ndjango-cors-headers 4.4.0 ships `default_headers` as exactly `accept,\nauthorization, content-type, user-agent, x-csrftoken, x-requested-with` —\nneither `if-match` nor `if-none-match`. The middleware never echoes the\nbrowser''s `Access-Control-Request-Headers`; it emits the configured tuple\nverbatim. So the preflight is refused:\n\n    Access to XMLHttpRequest at\n    ''http://localhost:8001/api/v1/trial-search-preferences/upsert/''\n    blocked by CORS policy: Request header field if-match is not allowed\n    by Access-Control-Allow-Headers in preflight response.\n\nThe request never leaves the browser. Nothing reaches this service, so\nnothing is in its logs; the client sees a network error rather than a status\ncode, and a client that treats a failed save as \"try again later\" shows the\nreader nothing at all.\n\nThe reach is wider than one endpoint. EXACT''s filter saves are always\nconditional — `If-Match` with the row''s tag, `If-None-Match: *` for the first\nwrite — so saved trial-search filters have not been savable from a browser\nfor as long as the feature has existed. (Not literally every write it makes:\nthe weights-wizard flag is deliberately unconditional, and the transport\nfalls back to no precondition when the tag is unknown. Those got through; the\nfilter saves are the ones that did not.) Measured in a browser against the\nlocal stack, not reasoned: person 9009''s row still held `preferences: {}`\nwith an `updated_at` four days old after a save the page reported as done.\n\nNot environment-specific. Both tuples are literals in `settings.py`, so it is\nevery deployment.\n\n`curl` is unaffected because it is not a browser and performs no preflight at\nall — which is why #1312''s endpoint tests and every manual check passed. Note\nwhat this is NOT: the unconditional PATCH already triggered a preflight too\n(`Content-Type: application/json` is not a safelisted value). The preflight\nwas always happening; it was the header list that refused it.\n\n`ETag` joins `CORS_EXPOSE_HEADERS` for the same reason one step later: a\nresponse header a cross-origin caller cannot read may as well not be sent.\nEXACT prefers the tag the body carries (`updated_at`, quoted) and treats the\nheader as its fallback, so this degraded rather than broke — but the fallback\nwas unreachable from a browser, including on the 412 path where it is the\nrecovery route for a proxy that strips bodies. It discloses nothing: the same\nresponse body already carries `updated_at` verbatim, which is why the tag is\nthat value and not a hash.\n\nTwo tests ask the settings, one asks the middleware, and one keeps the route\nhonest — `CorsMiddleware` answers a preflight before url resolution and\nwithout reading the requested-headers list, so the preflight test alone\npasses against a route that does not exist.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"e10753aa1eace42436c5ac59f047f31e28a21b29","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/git/trees/e10753aa1eace42436c5ac59f047f31e28a21b29"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/git/commits/ea6aa91bfc847e05e68a8e9f67582ddbdc717c61","verification":{"payload":"tree e10753aa1eace42436c5ac59f047f31e28a21b29\nparent e00ae1c4a60c31371091923cd679d2b83892b2b6\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1790344592 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790344592 -0400\n\nLet a browser make a conditional write (#1602)\n\nConditional writes went in with #1312 and have never worked from a browser.\n`CORS_ALLOW_HEADERS` is `default_headers` plus two provenance headers, and\ndjango-cors-headers 4.4.0 ships `default_headers` as exactly `accept,\nauthorization, content-type, user-agent, x-csrftoken, x-requested-with` —\nneither `if-match` nor `if-none-match`. The middleware never echoes the\nbrowser''s `Access-Control-Request-Headers`; it emits the configured tuple\nverbatim. So the preflight is refused:\n\n    Access to XMLHttpRequest at\n    ''http://localhost:8001/api/v1/trial-search-preferences/upsert/''\n    blocked by CORS policy: Request header field if-match is not allowed\n    by Access-Control-Allow-Headers in preflight response.\n\nThe request never leaves the browser. Nothing reaches this service, so\nnothing is in its logs; the client sees a network error rather than a status\ncode, and a client that treats a failed save as \"try again later\" shows the\nreader nothing at all.\n\nThe reach is wider than one endpoint. EXACT''s filter saves are always\nconditional — `If-Match` with the row''s tag, `If-None-Match: *` for the first\nwrite — so saved trial-search filters have not been savable from a browser\nfor as long as the feature has existed. (Not literally every write it makes:\nthe weights-wizard flag is deliberately unconditional, and the transport\nfalls back to no precondition when the tag is unknown. Those got through; the\nfilter saves are the ones that did not.) Measured in a browser against the\nlocal stack, not reasoned: person 9009''s row still held `preferences: {}`\nwith an `updated_at` four days old after a save the page reported as done.\n\nNot environment-specific. Both tuples are literals in `settings.py`, so it is\nevery deployment.\n\n`curl` is unaffected because it is not a browser and performs no preflight at\nall — which is why #1312''s endpoint tests and every manual check passed. Note\nwhat this is NOT: the unconditional PATCH already triggered a preflight too\n(`Content-Type: application/json` is not a safelisted value). The preflight\nwas always happening; it was the header list that refused it.\n\n`ETag` joins `CORS_EXPOSE_HEADERS` for the same reason one step later: a\nresponse header a cross-origin caller cannot read may as well not be sent.\nEXACT prefers the tag the body carries (`updated_at`, quoted) and treats the\nheader as its fallback, so this degraded rather than broke — but the fallback\nwas unreachable from a browser, including on the 412 path where it is the\nrecovery route for a proxy that strips bodies. It discloses nothing: the same\nresponse body already carries `updated_at` verbatim, which is why the tag is\nthat value and not a hash.\n\nTwo tests ask the settings, one asks the middleware, and one keeps the route\nhonest — `CorsMiddleware` answers a preflight before url resolution and\nwithout reading the requested-headers list, so the preflight test alone\npasses against a route that does not exist.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtn2QCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAABSoQAEAGsqBhYKIjvKJABJ5LbwxC\n+o2fkqtY0LKmolwYNfKPkKckQHAfd6k+oVU4SvmzcTcaNrM78nVQUj2U9kt1YZX4\nsKxnNoE7tzf4xf7Y+NMo6n1l5EiXh8mlBDlxbhdE+7SL+JEAJ4CQM9gX59wbaGK6\nP8h6CCVcy/b3PQ3v/l6tsqeiVtQJ/wikb6x40gIYBYRBz02WFpKwFxdiHzn4JedN\nEczV8hEkYE+wlkI3D+WrqBQ0r15IEC2f9FgJCH3zTM7v3CrVEko19q7hbcPyWXz4\n3ciW53B929GNB6LYk9zyBckOAO2UVE4IE1cJ8kTDHhGZLe6N+J391SJLz1tBuMXC\nJ595O8qNYXNytXs6gWrf0mjcTsSRgeBLWYdcoqaQax1Nv62NqpRXij+VY4ykF6L8\nkCl7uwjkIrQQiM1lpZBtxUDesNkbMFV/7SZX17VmdnTtUcmfxz6ZeAkFPpMB6q6P\nHUyFp2z/4yObtWNBoZFyUOi2vFn3pLEWp3OK/wDjDt9dg9dP4JxqRU0Udb9iBXyZ\nVJDDrNa+CNoS96KtI/l6NkS98vldaMSicorBIBm/c3KokWCA1SQ/T3shERlWGemq\nO8koufibO8obMoKNZn+8VsioIcracuT/SZAajTwa46rQKhnOvyD+tCK9QWeOGNve\n/PRNxK7R2VnEm3iBiVel\n=Gz/Y\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-25T13:56:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/promop/commit/ea6aa91bfc847e05e68a8e9f67582ddbdc717c61","node_id":"C_kwDOPw6uhdoAKGVhNmFhOTFiZmM4NDdlMDVlNjhhOGU5ZjY3NTgyZGRiZGM3MTdjNjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/promop/commit/e00ae1c4a60c31371091923cd679d2b83892b2b6","sha":"e00ae1c4a60c31371091923cd679d2b83892b2b6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/commits/e00ae1c4a60c31371091923cd679d2b83892b2b6"}],"sha":"ea6aa91bfc847e05e68a8e9f67582ddbdc717c61","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/commits/ea6aa91bfc847e05e68a8e9f67582ddbdc717c61"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/commits/e00ae1c4a60c31371091923cd679d2b83892b2b6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T13:55:50Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T13:55:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Record whether a patient has been offered the weights wizard (#1606)\n\nThe trial-search page is going to offer a first-time patient a short wizard\nthat ranks the four suitability factors and writes 40/30/20/10. Offering it\nagain to someone who said no is the whole problem, so somewhere has to\nremember that we asked. EXACT is stateless about patients; this row already\nholds their filters.\n\nA column beside `preferences`, not a key inside it, for two reasons that are\neach sufficient and both tested:\n\n- `reset` sets `preferences` to `{}` wholesale, so a flag living in there\n  would be cleared by the Reset filters button and the patient asked again\n  for having pressed it.\n- `non_default_filter_count` counts keys in that payload and drives the UI''s\n  \"Filters (N)\" badge, so a truthy key would tick it up by one for everyone\n  who has ever been asked.\n\n## One-way, and where that is enforced\n\nNot in a validator. Two reviews took that apart:\n\n`ModelSerializer.update` calls a bare `instance.save()`, which writes every\ncolumn from the copy this request read. The unconditional write path — the\ndefault, since `If-Match` is opt-in — takes no lock and runs in no\ntransaction. So a filter save that began before the offer landed wrote the\nstale `False` back afterwards, with no 412 and nothing in the response to say\nso, and the patient was asked again. Its body never mentioned the flag. A\nvalidator cannot see this: the stale row it would compare against is the same\nstale row.\n\nSo `update` writes the column only when the body carries it, and only ever to\n`True`. There is no request that can clear it.\n\nA body carrying `false` is ignored rather than refused. A 400 was written and\nwithdrawn: this endpoint''s documented flow is read-modify-write, so a client\nholding a representation read before the offer sends `false` back on its next\nFILTER save without meaning anything by it — and refusing that lost the filter\nedit, and kept losing it until the client happened to re-read. Ignoring it costs almost nothing, because the\nvalue it asks for is the one value the column will not take. Almost: a body\ncarrying only `weights_wizard_offered: false` still saves `updated_at`, so it\nmoves the ETag and can 412 a concurrent conditional writer. Measured. Not a\nregression — an empty `{}` body does the same — but \"nothing\" was too strong.\n\n`update_fields` names `updated_at` explicitly, because it narrows what\n`auto_now` refreshes: leaving it out would freeze the ETag and let a client\nhold a representation of a row that has moved.\n\n## What the response describes, and the invariant behind it\n\nThree review rounds each found a different defect in this one method, and\neach fix broke the next case along. They are all violations of one rule that\nwas never written down, so it is written down now, in the code:\n\n    the body and the ETag must describe ONE row version.\n\nBoth are rendered from the same instance, so it reduces to: every field the\nresponse shows must come from the same moment. Three ways to try, and only\nthe last holds.\n\nRender what this request wrote, re-reading nothing — how it shipped first.\nWrong as soon as the save is narrow: a flag-only PATCH leaves `preferences`\nunwritten, so the 200 pairs a post-write flag with a pre-read payload and\nstamps it with a currently valid ETag. A client doing the documented\nread-modify-write then spends that tag as `If-Match`, is told the precondition\nholds, and overwrites content it never read — verbatim the failure\n`partial_update` avoids by not re-reading at all. Measured, two requests,\nEXACT''s own shape: B''s body came back `{''a'': 1}` against a database holding\n`{''a'': 1, ''b'': 2}`, and B''s next write dropped `b`. The bare `save()` this\nmethod replaces made the body true by force, so the gap arrived with the\nnarrow save.\n\nRe-read only the columns the save skipped — the second attempt. Also wrong: a\nwriter landing between the save and the re-read puts THEIR value in the body\nbeside OUR `updated_at`, and the client''s next conditional write 412s having\nchanged nothing.\n\nRe-read the whole row in one SELECT, and only when the save left something\nbehind. Body and tag then come from one snapshot whichever way the race went.\nThe cost is that the response may describe a version this request did not\nproduce — which is what a GET a millisecond later would have said.\n\n`reset` needed the same rule and, in the first version of this, did not get\nit — the invariant was stated in one place and applied in one of the two that\nneeded it, which review caught immediately. It writes in the view rather than\nthrough the serializer, so it carries its own whole-row re-read now.\n\nThree tests pin the three wrong versions: one drives the serializer with a\nstale instance, and two force a writer INTO the re-read — the one moment that\nmatters and the one a thread pair cannot be made to hit.\n\nAnd the column is read back when the request did not SET it — which includes\nthe body that carried `false`, not only the body that said nothing. Four\nreview rounds peeled the same staleness apart in layers, and the last two were\nthis one: the row was being saved correctly and then described wrongly.\n`instance` still held the value this request read, so a filter save that\noverlapped the offer answered `weights_wizard_offered: false` beside the ETag\nof a row in which it is true, and a client that decides from its own write''s\nresponse rather than from a fresh read offered the wizard again. The guard was\nfirst written `if offered is None`, which skipped exactly the\nread-modify-write client the paragraph above is about — its row was protected\nand its response was not.\n\nThe write-once contract is declared on the serializer, where a client can see\nit and where re-wording costs no `AlterField`. Nothing else in the payload\nsaid that a `false` is accepted and ignored.\n\n`reset` needed the same re-read and does not go through the serializer at all\n— it writes in the view. Only its unconditional branch: the conditional one\nreads under `select_for_update`, so no writer can move the flag while it holds\nthe row — on PostgreSQL. On the SQLite dev fallback that is a no-op and the\nguarantee degrades to best-effort, like every other precondition in the file.\n\n## The shared concurrency contract\n\nThe flag uses the row''s existing one rather than a write path of its own.\nSetting it bumps the ETag, so a concurrent filter save may need one retry —\nthe cheaper of the two failures, since a targeted update that left the\nvalidator alone would let a client cache a representation with the wrong flag,\nand would make `If-Match` mean different things depending on which endpoint\nwrote the row. Setting it twice is idempotent, so a retry after a 412 need not\nknow whether its first attempt landed.\n\nFinishing the wizard can be one PATCH carrying both the weights and the flag;\ndeclining is one PATCH carrying the flag alone. But `preferences` is replaced\nWHOLESALE, so that combined write owes a read-modify-write: a body carrying\nonly the weights deletes whatever filters the patient had. The migration\ndefaults every existing row to not-yet-offered, so the wizard IS offered to\npeople with saved filters, which makes this the common case rather than an\nedge one. Both shapes are pinned — the merging one, and the one that loses\nthe filters — because the shape that loses them is the shape that looks\nright. (EXACT does not take that route at all: its weights go through the\nsame merging writer as every other filter change, and the flag follows as a\nseparate unconditional PATCH.)\n\n## Notes\n\nA threaded version of the revert race was written and removed: two barriers\ncannot force the interleave that matters — the offer has to land between the\nfilter request''s read and its write — so it passed against the broken code.\nThe deterministic pair that drives the serializer with a stale instance\nreproduces the same state and does fail against it.\n\n`can_write_patient` admits staff, service tokens, representatives, doctors and\norg admins, so a non-patient identity can set this flag for someone and no API\npath undoes it: no request clears it, `reset` does not touch it, the viewset\nexposes no DELETE, and the model is in no `admin.py`. The only remedy is a\nshell against the database, and nothing records who set it. Inherited from the\nrow, not introduced here, and the cost is small — somebody silently never sees\nan optional wizard — but it is a real one-way door, so it is now written where\na reader lands (the serializer''s `update` docstring) rather than only here.\n\nVerified with pytest: the preferences suite 113 passing, and the full suite\n3584 passing. The one failure,\n`test_packaged_artifacts_match_frozen_migration_manifest`, and the Django\nrunner''s \"Publisher source catalog checksum mismatch: NCIt\" both reproduce on\nclean `dev` — unresolved Git LFS pointers in the checkout, fixed by\n`git lfs pull`, unrelated to this change. `makemigrations --check` reports no\nchanges.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2449ff86054ef7fcdf63f6988bf34defb16a1c59","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/git/trees/2449ff86054ef7fcdf63f6988bf34defb16a1c59"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/promop/git/commits/e0