\n`{''a'': 1, ''b'': 2}`, and B''s next write dropped `b`. The bare `save()` this\nmethod replaces made the body true by force, so the gap arrived with the\nnarrow save.\n\nRe-read only the columns the save skipped — the second attempt. Also wrong: a\nwriter landing between the save and the re-read puts THEIR value in the body\nbeside OUR `updated_at`, and the client''s next conditional write 412s having\nchanged nothing.\n\nRe-read the whole row in one SELECT, and only when the save left something\nbehind. Body and tag then come from one snapshot whichever way the race went.\nThe cost is that the response may describe a version this request did not\nproduce — which is what a GET a millisecond later would have said.\n\n`reset` needed the same rule and, in the first version of this, did not get\nit — the invariant was stated in one place and applied in one of the two that\nneeded it, which review caught immediately. It writes in the view rather than\nthrough the serializer, so it carries its own whole-row re-read now.\n\nThree tests pin the three wrong versions: one drives the serializer with a\nstale instance, and two force a writer INTO the re-read — the one moment that\nmatters and the one a thread pair cannot be made to hit.\n\nAnd the column is read back when the request did not SET it — which includes\nthe body that carried `false`, not only the body that said nothing. Four\nreview rounds peeled the same staleness apart in layers, and the last two were\nthis one: the row was being saved correctly and then described wrongly.\n`instance` still held the value this request read, so a filter save that\noverlapped the offer answered `weights_wizard_offered: false` beside the ETag\nof a row in which it is true, and a client that decides from its own write''s\nresponse rather than from a fresh read offered the wizard again. The guard was\nfirst written `if offered is None`, which skipped exactly the\nread-modify-write client the paragraph above is about — its row was protected\nand its response was not.\n\nThe write-once contract is declared on the serializer, where a client can see\nit and where re-wording costs no `AlterField`. Nothing else in the payload\nsaid that a `false` is accepted and ignored.\n\n`reset` needed the same re-read and does not go through the serializer at all\n— it writes in the view. Only its unconditional branch: the conditional one\nreads under `select_for_update`, so no writer can move the flag while it holds\nthe row — on PostgreSQL. On the SQLite dev fallback that is a no-op and the\nguarantee degrades to best-effort, like every other precondition in the file.\n\n## The shared concurrency contract\n\nThe flag uses the row''s existing one rather than a write path of its own.\nSetting it bumps the ETag, so a concurrent filter save may need one retry —\nthe cheaper of the two failures, since a targeted update that left the\nvalidator alone would let a client cache a representation with the wrong flag,\nand would make `If-Match` mean different things depending on which endpoint\nwrote the row. Setting it twice is idempotent, so a retry after a 412 need not\nknow whether its first attempt landed.\n\nFinishing the wizard can be one PATCH carrying both the weights and the flag;\ndeclining is one PATCH carrying the flag alone. But `preferences` is replaced\nWHOLESALE, so that combined write owes a read-modify-write: a body carrying\nonly the weights deletes whatever filters the patient had. The migration\ndefaults every existing row to not-yet-offered, so the wizard IS offered to\npeople with saved filters, which makes this the common case rather than an\nedge one. Both shapes are pinned — the merging one, and the one that loses\nthe filters — because the shape that loses them is the shape that looks\nright. (EXACT does not take that route at all: its weights go through the\nsame merging writer as every other filter change, and the flag follows as a\nseparate unconditional PATCH.)\n\n## Notes\n\nA threaded version of the revert race was written and removed: two barriers\ncannot force the interleave that matters — the offer has to land between the\nfilter request''s read and its write — so it passed against the broken code.\nThe deterministic pair that drives the serializer with a stale instance\nreproduces the same state and does fail against it.\n\n`can_write_patient` admits staff, service tokens, representatives, doctors and\norg admins, so a non-patient identity can set this flag for someone and no API\npath undoes it: no request clears it, `reset` does not touch it, the viewset\nexposes no DELETE, and the model is in no `admin.py`. The only remedy is a\nshell against the database, and nothing records who set it. Inherited from the\nrow, not introduced here, and the cost is small — somebody silently never sees\nan optional wizard — but it is a real one-way door, so it is now written where\na reader lands (the serializer''s `update` docstring) rather than only here.\n\nVerified with pytest: the preferences suite 113 passing, and the full suite\n3584 passing. The one failure,\n`test_packaged_artifacts_match_frozen_migration_manifest`, and the Django\nrunner''s \"Publisher source catalog checksum mismatch: NCIt\" both reproduce on\nclean `dev` — unresolved Git LFS pointers in the checkout, fixed by\n`git lfs pull`, unrelated to this change. `makemigrations --check` reports no\nchanges.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtn1nCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAe8UQAA32yEON+0wbBBudgXHAj5NO\n8/0tMyXX94rq+bsrEjqeIOYKdVJoH9fJ6aeuW67s1elQSJdrmJvMswQcTI+0djHf\nIm+QKhBEo1Z35dHtSq7HziHVYn9kyt8HnxadZ72d6KWAHPoYHFSq3GVUv6qVDR2+\ni5yakyL5szMdjmbj4RjSSFsHkM4kM8ch9AV8E5pQdt/7d3yhRoX/Ex0VKYSK4itA\nr3Ntv5HFmSuMCq1k6bwtIHgW+MUB22SJxk983YOrHVZStTBgmDLEs7O8Efce16v0\nFnQ1UTZ5BmaCjdUvr+es0ysoLPF+6Un70tCugZUERQEeJN2sOAzTJiqZy5N0PDr7\n9JdkU33TCxk2315XKHPlz+exmEioxxBQ7ndvyZro8icPSn+AZOgtzz1IiT5DdpAq\nyJRRPqtfCYlL